(3144437, 434696, 52336, '<td valign="top">Very cool repeating patterns.  I think the high contrast makes it, and adds to the sparseness of the subject versus the richness of its content.  I find myself really looking for all the different types of patterns, in the screen, the wood grain, the mouldings, the old paint.</td>', 'Very cool repeating patterns.  I think the high contrast makes it, and adds to the sparseness of the subject versus the richness of its content.  I find myself really looking for all the different types of patterns, in the screen, the wood grain, the mouldings, the old paint.', '2006-12-23 11:33:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(3145898, 436649, 68546, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by PGerst:</b></div><hr/><i> A little out of focus. I would have preferred a greater DOF. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks for the comment, the DOF was a kept shallow on purpose as to take away as much distraction as possible and focus wholey on one of the coins. So i adjusted down to until i had just the whole 20pence piece in the center completely within the dof and left it there. I think this would appear very busy if everything was within the dof<br/>thanks</td>', 'Originally posted by PGerst: A little out of focus. I would have preferred a greater DOF.   Thanks for the comment, the DOF was a kept shallow on purpose as to take away as much distraction as possible and focus wholey on one of the coins. So i adjusted down to until i had just the whole 20pence piece in the center completely within the dof and left it there. I think this would appear very busy if everything was within the dofthanks', '2006-12-24 04:58:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(3150667, 436903, 55838, '<td valign="top">Congrats on top 20 Phil - great image!</td>', 'Congrats on top 20 Phil - great image!', '2006-12-26 20:58:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(3152028, 436416, 30982, '<td valign="top">I loved it for this challenge - what a refreshing take on a dirty subject!  And for the record, I recognized the parking lot stripe right away.  Either I have a good monitor, or I happen to look at parking lots alot (which I do - the textures in those painted stripes can be fascinating in their own way.)  Nice work; ignore scores.</td>', 'I loved it for this challenge - what a refreshing take on a dirty subject!  And for the record, I recognized the parking lot stripe right away.  Either I have a good monitor, or I happen to look at parking lots alot (which I do - the textures in those painted stripes can be fascinating in their own way.)  Nice work; ignore scores.', '2006-12-27 01:52:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(3153832, 437016, 811, '<td valign="top">I am no help when it comes to giving punch in post processing, but maybe i can help compositionally.  You, did, however, meet the challenge really well.  That is definitely dirt~<br/> <br/>I think if you could have stood a few feet more to your left so that the road was entering in the lower corner instead of mid frame, it would have given the viewer a place to "start." (or even in the road if there was no traffic)  As it is, the dirt pile in front becomes the first place the viewer''s eyes rest, and as it is not terribly interesting, there isn''t a huge degree of motivation to keep looking.  However, here is where the overcast helped you I think.  Because the sky is brighter than the rest, the eyes are drawn upwards, and then find the road and follow it.<br/> <br/>That road is just begging for a dirt bike or kid on a bicycle to stir up some big old dust clouds.  You know like when you ride really fast and lock the brakes and turn in a circle.</td>', 'I am no help when it comes to giving punch in post processing, but maybe i can help compositionally.  You, did, however, meet the challenge really well.  That is definitely dirt~ I think if you could have stood a few feet more to your left so that the road was entering in the lower corner instead of mid frame, it would have given the viewer a place to "start." (or even in the road if there was no traffic)  As it is, the dirt pile in front becomes the first place the viewer''s eyes rest, and as it is not terribly interesting, there isn''t a huge degree of motivation to keep looking.  However, here is where the overcast helped you I think.  Because the sky is brighter than the rest, the eyes are drawn upwards, and then find the road and follow it. That road is just begging for a dirt bike or kid on a bicycle to stir up some big old dust clouds.  You know like when you ride really fast and lock the brakes and turn in a circle.', '2006-12-27 15:06:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(3155528, 436848, 52467, '<td valign="top">well done Deb..Great job on the processing.</td>', 'well done Deb..Great job on the processing.', '2006-12-28 08:13:17.000000', NULL, 0),
